:lm margin=1.
:h1 res=10001.The plot
:i1.The plot
:p.
The aim of the game is to :link reftype=hd res=10002.guide :elink. :hp2.Ribble:ehp2.
:artwork name='..\bitmaps\repr1.bmp' align=left runin. around a number
of levels, collecting diamonds, killing monsters, and
solving puzzles.
:p.
The items found on the levels are divided into three groups&colon.
:lm margin=5.
:p.
&sqbul. :link reftype=hd res=10004.Collectables :elink.
.br
&sqbul. :link reftype=hd res=10005.Monsters :elink.
.br
&sqbul. :link reftype=hd res=10007.Miscellaneous Items :elink.
.br
:lm margin=1.
:p.
Each level must be completed within a certain time.  The time
remaining is displayed at the top of the playing area along with
your :link reftype=hd res=10015.score :elink., number of lives, and the number of
diamonds remaining on the current level.
:p.
The value for the number of diamonds remaining indicates how
many diamonds, safes, and cages remain to be collected.
:h1 res=10002.Keys
:i1 id=keyshelp.Keys
:p.
To move :hp2.Ribble:ehp2. around, use the following keys&colon.
:lm margin=5.
:p.
:hp2.Up cursor:ehp2. key, to move up
.br
.br
:hp2.Down Cursor:ehp2. key, to move down
.br
.br
:hp2.Left Cursor:ehp2. key, to move left
.br
.br
:hp2.Right Cursor:ehp2. key, to move right
.br
.br
:hp2.Escape:ehp2. key, to kill yourself, and restart the current level
from scratch.
.br
.br
:hp2.Space:ehp2. key, to toggle the map display on/off
:lm margin=1.
:p.
Other keys are described in the on-line help sections
for the areas where they are applicable.
:h2 res=10011.Help for keys menu-item
:p.
Select the :hp2.Keys:ehp2. menu-item for help relating
to keys.
:h1 res=10016.Goodies&comma. Monsters&comma. and other Things
:i1 id=mapitems.Goodies&comma. Monsters&comma. and other Things
:p.
Items found on the various levels&colon.
:lm margin=5.
:p.
&sqbul. :link reftype=hd res=10004.Collectables :elink.
.br
&sqbul. :link reftype=hd res=10005.Monsters :elink.
.br
&sqbul. :link reftype=hd res=10007.Miscellaneous Items :elink.
.br
:lm margin=1.
:h2 res=10004.Collectables
:i2.Collectables
:p.
These are the collectable items that can be found scattered around
the levels&colon.
:lm margin=1.
:p.
:artwork name='..\bitmaps\earth.bmp' align=left runin.
Earth
:lm margin=5.
:p.
Earth is found on just about all the levels.  It acts as a support
for boulders and other droppable objects.
:lm margin=1.
:p.
:artwork name='..\bitmaps\diamond.bmp' align=left runin.
Diamond
:lm margin=5.
:p.
:hp2.Ribble:ehp2.'s primary goal is to collect all the diamonds on each level.
Diamonds are either found in this form, or are created by spirits
entering cages, or by collecting a key to unlock the safes.  More
on these later.
:lm margin=1.
:p.
:artwork name='..\bitmaps\key.bmp' align=left runin.
Key
:lm margin=5.
:p.
When :hp2.Ribble:ehp2. picks up a key, all the safes :artwork name='..\bitmaps\safe.bmp' align=left runin.
on the current level are converted into diamonds which can then be collected.
Beware of boulders or other droppable objects perched on top of safes - they
might slide off if the safe changes into a diamond.
:lm margin=1.
:h2 res=10005.Monsters
:i2.Monsters
:p.
These are the monsters which you'll be unfortunate enough
to encounter during your wanderings through the various
levels&colon.
:p.
:lm margin=1.
:artwork name='..\bitmaps\egg.bmp' align=left runin.
Egg
:lm margin=5.
:p.
If you dislodge an egg, then it will crack and eventually
a Slither monster will hatch out.
:p.
Eggs are like boulders in that they will slide off curved
surfaces.  They also count as curved objects, so boulders
and other eggs will slide off them.  Eggs don't crack if
something is dropped on them.
:lm margin=1.
:p.
:artwork name='..\bitmaps\crackedegg.bmp' align=left runin.
Cracked egg
:lm margin=5.
:p.
After an egg has been dislodged, there is a short period
during which the Slither monster breaks out of the egg.
:lm margin=1.
:p.
:artwork name='..\bitmaps\slither.bmp' align=left runin.
Slither
:lm margin=5.
:p.
At the end of the hatching period, a fully grown Slither emerges
from its egg, and begins its sole purpose in life - your death.
:p.
A Slither can only be killed by a boulder.  Either by having
one dropped on its head, or having one pushed into it.
:lm margin=1.
:p.
:artwork name='..\bitmaps\spirit.bmp' align=left runin.
Spirit
:lm margin=5.
:p.
Spirits are small fluffy creatures which wander
sightlessly around the levels.  They are deadly to touch,
and must be carefully shepherded into cages
:artwork name='..\bitmaps\cage.bmp' align=left runin. where they
will then turn into diamonds.
:h2 res=10007.Miscellaneous items
:i2.Miscellaneous items
:p.
These are the miscellaneous items that can be found scattered around
the levels&colon.
:p.
:artwork name='..\bitmaps\boulder.bmp' align=left runin.
Boulder
:lm margin=5.
:p.
Boulders can be dropped by removing whatever's supporting them,
or by pushing them. They slide off curved surfaces (including eggs
and diamonds), and can be used to kill some types of monster.
Being hit by a falling boulder is usually fatal.
:lm margin=1.
:p.
:artwork name='..\bitmaps\teleport.bmp' align=left runin.
Teleporter
:lm margin=5.
:p.
When :hp2.Ribble:ehp2. walks into a teleporter, he is teleported to a different
location on the current level.  Once used, teleporters vanish.
:lm margin=1.
:p.
:artwork name='..\bitmaps\cage.bmp' align=left runin.
Cage
:lm margin=5.
:p.
Guide the Spirits into cages, and they will turn into diamonds.  In order
to complete a level, each cage must have been converted into a diamond.
:lm margin=1.
:p.
:artwork name='..\bitmaps\safe.bmp' align=left runin.
Safe
:lm margin=5.
:p.
When :hp2.Ribble:ehp2. picks up the key :artwork name='..\bitmaps\key.bmp' align=left runin., all the
safes on the current level are converted into diamonds.  As with the cages,
you must have collected all the safe diamonds in order to complete a level.
:lm margin=1.
:p.
:artwork name='..\bitmaps\bomb.bmp' align=left runin.
Bomb
:lm margin=5.
:p.
Bombs become live when they are dropped onto something other than
earth :artwork name='..\bitmaps\earth.bmp' align=left runin.&per.  Once live
:artwork name='..\bitmaps\bombli1.bmp' align=left runin., you have about
five seconds get out of the way before a bomb detonates
:artwork name='..\bitmaps\flame1.bmp' align=left runin.&per.
:lm margin=1.
:h2 res=10015.Scoring
:i2.Scoring
:p.
Points are gained for collecting and killing things on the levels, as follows&colon.
:p.
:artwork name='..\bitmaps\earth.bmp' align=left runin. 1 point
:p.
:artwork name='..\bitmaps\diamond.bmp' align=left runin. 10 points
:p.
:artwork name='..\bitmaps\key.bmp' align=left runin. 10 points
:p.
:artwork name='..\bitmaps\spirit.bmp' align=left runin. + :artwork name='..\bitmaps\cage.bmp' align=left runin. 25 points
:p.
:artwork name='..\bitmaps\slither.bmp' align=left runin. + :artwork name='..\bitmaps\boulder.bmp' align=left runin.  50 points
:h1 res=10018.Selecting levels
:i1 id=selectlevel.Selecting levels
:p.
When you complete a level, you automatically progress onto the next
one.  If you want go straight to a particular level, then you
can use the :link reftype=hd res=10012.level selection dialog :elink. brought up
when you select the
:hp2.Open...:ehp2. menu-item under the :hp2.Game:ehp2. menu.
:h2 res=10012.Open... level dialog
:i2.Open... level dialog
:p.
Use this dialog to select the level you wish to play.
:h1 res=10019.Editing levels
:i1.Editing levels
:p.
Select the :hp2.Level...:ehp2. menu-item under the :hp2.Map Editor:ehp2. submenu of the
:hp2.Options:ehp2. menu at
any time while you are playing a level. The :hp2.Ribble:ehp2. window will
expand horizontally, and a palette of all the available game items
will appear on the right-hand side of the window.
:p.
The :hp2.Ribble:ehp2. will be replaced by a cross-hair cursor
:artwork name='..\bitmaps\edit.bmp' align=center runin. which indicates which
map cell you want to change.  The usual :hp2.Ribble:ehp2. keys apply
while editing, and all creatures are halted.
:p.
:hp1.Adding items to the playing area:ehp1.
:lm margin=3.
:p.
Click on an item in the palette to select it.  The currently
selected palette item is displayed next to the :hp2.Current >>:ehp2.
caption above the palette.
:p.
Pressing the :hp2.Enter:ehp2. key will place the selected item under
the cross-hair in the playing area.
:p.
When you place a creature onto the map, it doesn't become alive
until you leave edit mode.
:lm margin=1.
:p.
:hp1.Removing creatures from the map:ehp1.
:lm margin=3.
:p.
Position the cross-hair so that it overlaps the creature, and
press the :hp2.Delete:ehp2. key.  The creature will be removed.
:p.
Note that items such as Spirits and Teleporters which you
place on the playing area don't become creatures as such
until you leave edit mode.
:lm margin=1.
:p.
:hp1.Setting teleport destinations:ehp1.
:lm margin=3.
:p.
Each teleporter has a destination location associated with it.
These destination locations are marked with a white number on
a blue background, and are overlaid on whatever item is at
the destination.
:p.
In addition to the location marker, teleporters
also have a white on red number stamped on them so that you can
tell which destination marker is associated with which teleporter.
:p.
To move a destination marker, position the cross-hard above it,
and press the :hp2.Insert:ehp2. key.  If you now move the cross-hair,
the number will follow.  Press the :hp2.Return:ehp2. key to drop the
number in the new location.
:lm margin=1.
:p.
See Also&colon.
:lm margin=5.
:p.
&sqbul. :link reftype=hd res=10020.Saving levels :elink.
:lm margin=1.
:h1 res=10020.Saving levels
:i1 id=saving.Saving levels
:p.
While playing or editing a level, you can save the current
playing area to a level file.
:p.
Use the :hp2.Save...:ehp2. menu-item under the :hp2.Game:ehp2.
menu to bring up a :link reftype=hd res=10021.dialog :elink. with which you can save your
level.
:h2 res=10021.Save level dialog
:i2.Save level dialog
:p.
This dialog allows you fill in various bits of information
about the level you are about to save.
:p.
Click the :hp2.OK:ehp2. button to save the level and associated
information, or the :hp2.Cancel:ehp2. to close the dialog without
saving the level.
:p.
Note the level number spin-button at the bottom of the dialog.
This number indicates the position in the level playing sequence.
In order for :hp2.Ribble:ehp2. to use your levels they
must be numbered sequentially from one (1) upwards.  You
can use the level selection dialog to find out the current
highest numbered level, or look in the "levels" directory
contained in the :hp2.Ribble:ehp2. executable's directory.
