:h2 res=10013.System requirements and notes
:i2.System requirements and notes
:p.
:hp2.Ribble:ehp2. is both a CPU and graphics intensive game.  The playing
area is updated many times a second, so, in order for :hp2.Ribble:ehp2.
to run acceptably on your machine, you should take note of the
following points&colon.
:p.
The faster your processor, the better.  :hp2.Ribble:ehp2. was written on
a 486DX33, 486DX50, and P60, so performance on these processors
is satisfactory (particularly the last one!).
:p.
Since the rendering technique employed by :hp2.Ribble:ehp2. involves blitting
a largish bitmap to the screen many times a second, :hp2.Ribble:ehp2. will
run considerably better on systems with Local Bus or PCI graphics cards
than on those with (even accelerated) ISA cards.  Both 486's used in
:hp2.Ribble:ehp2.'s development had ISA cards, and the P60 had a #9GXE
thingy + PCI bus.
:p.
You can tune performance by selecting the :hp2.Options...:ehp2. menu-item
under the :hp2.Map Editor:ehp2. submenu of the :hp2.Options:ehp2. menu and altering
the settings contained inside
the :link reftype=hd res=10023.dialog :elink. that is displayed.
:h2 res=10003.Licencing
:i2.Licencing
:artwork name='..\bitmaps\tribble-normal.bmp' align=center.
:p.
:hp2.Ribble:ehp2. is Tribbleware.  This means that if you like it,
and play it while your boss ducks out for a quick
smoke, then you should feel under some kind of
obligation to send us one of those furry Tribble
things with the antennae, wobbly eyes, and an amusing
motto for a tail... Failing that, a postcard would be nice.
:p.
Users interested in designing better graphics, levels,
or adding other features into the game are welcome to
get in touch.  Irritating little .:hp2.MID:ehp2. ditties which we can
run off MMPM/2 would be most useful.
